@*
    Index.cshtml — the ESC /admin/escalation outbound-config form (ADR 0159,
    ESC·4 / ESC·12).

    The origin GlobalAdmin's own outgoing settings: the receiving platform's
    inbound Endpoint (the escalation.outbound.endpoint.label field), the
    plaintext access Token the origin admin was handed out-of-band (the
    escalation.outbound.token.label field, rendered as a password input — the
    ESC·4 outgoing secret, **not** a secret of the receiving platform,
    contrast the receiving-side EscalationToken.TokenHash which is hash-only-
    at-rest, ESC·3), and an Enabled switch (the
    escalation.outbound.enabled.label field). When Enabled is false the M32·6
    env var KUMUNITA_ESCALATION_ENDPOINT remains the unauthenticated operator-
    override / fallback (the EscalationForwarder, U06).

    The [Authorize(Roles = GlobalAdmin)] gate is the standard admin surface
    (the ErrorReportAdminController shape, the ADR 0001-B thin-token rule);
    no new AccessAction / Decide() branch / IAuthorizationService surface
    (ESC·12). The save is the ESC·4 single-write-lane (the
    EscalationOutboundConfig doc + one AccessAudit row, Via = Admin, action
    escalation.outbound.save) in one write session (the ADR 0006 C3 shape, the
    ErrorReportService.MarkResolvedAsync precedent).

    The copy is the closed escalation.outbound.* kw-l key set (the U07 closed
    set, ESC·11 — the register's §2.3 keys 1–9). Every label goes through
    <kw-l> by key (the kw-l TagHelper floor, ADR 0015 D1 — an unregistered key
    falls back to the raw key, so the view renders the en reference text until
    U07 authors the values).

    Razor traps honored (AGENTS.md):
      - No <kw-l> inside a quoted attribute value (the field placeholders use
        the en source text as a literal attribute value, not a <kw-l> element
        inside a quoted attribute — AGENTS.md Razor trap #3).
      - No un-awaited Task<string> in markup (the flash strings are
        server-resolved by the controller's FlashAsync — the view only renders
        the resolved string).
      - No style=/data-* attributes built by interpolating quoted strings via
        @(...) (the Bootstrap classes are static strings).
*@
@model Kumunita.Web.Models.EscalationConfigFormModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Admin — Escalation settings";
}

@* The /admin section sub-nav (the ADR 0062 nav-tabs idiom): top of the page.
   This view sits in Views/Admin/Escalation/, so the shared partial — which
   lives in Views/Admin/ (the AdminController's own section) — must be
   referenced absolutely (the AdminStorage/Index.cshtml precedent). *@
@await Html.PartialAsync("~/Views/Admin/_AdminNav.cshtml")

<h1><kw-l key="escalation.title">Escalation</kw-l></h1>

<p class="lead">
    <kw-l key="escalation.intro">Escalation moves an issue from one Kumunita instance to another. This page sets up sending — where this instance forwards escalated issues and the token that authorizes it. The other direction, receiving, is managed on the Receiving access tokens page.</kw-l>
</p>

@* The "how it works" explainer (the escalation.how.* kw-l keys — the U07-style
   closed set this lane adds to the ESC·11 escalation.* set). This is the
   clarification the user asked for: the page mixes two directions, so the card
   names both and points to the sibling page that owns the other one. The
   "receiving" sentence is plain copy + a bare <a> (no <kw-l> inside an
   attribute — the AGENTS.md Razor trap #3; the link label is a separate
   <kw-l> element sitting outside the link's quoted attributes). The card is a
   static <section> with Bootstrap classes (no interpolated style=/data-* —
   the AGENTS.md Razor trap #2). *@
<div class="card kmb-escalation-how mb-4">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold">
        <kw-l key="escalation.how.heading">How escalation works</kw-l>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p class="mb-2">
            <kw-l key="escalation.how.outbound">This page is for sending: when an admin escalates a report from the Error reports page, it is forwarded to another instance's queue. Fill in the receiving instance's inbound endpoint and the access token its admin shared with you, then turn escalation on. While it is off, escalating a report does nothing.</kw-l>
        </p>
        <p class="mb-3">
            <kw-l key="escalation.how.inbound">The other direction is receiving: to let other instances escalate issues to this one, generate an access token for each of them and share it with their admin, who enters it on their own copy of this page.</kw-l>
            <a class="ms-1" href="/admin/escalation/tokens"><kw-l key="escalation.tokens.title">Receiving access tokens</kw-l> →</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

@* The success flash (the escalation.outbound.saved key, the U07 closed set,
   ESC·11) — rendered when the just-processed Save action succeeded (the
   Model.Saved flag, the register's "re-render with the Saved flag" shape). *@
@if (Model.Saved)
{
    <div class="alert alert-success">
        <kw-l key="escalation.outbound.saved">Escalation settings saved.</kw-l>
    </div>
}

@* The validation error flash (the M32·4 / M31·6 no-op shape — a blank or
   invalid Endpoint when Enabled is true). Server-resolved by the controller
   (the AdminOnboardingController.FlashAsync idiom). *@
@if (TempData["error"] is string err)
{
    <div class="alert alert-danger">@err</div>
}

@* The outbound-config form (the ESC·4 pin — the Endpoint + Token + Enabled
   fields). A [ValidateAntiForgeryToken] POST to /admin/escalation (the
   register's "re-render with the Saved flag" shape). Server-side validation
   is the gate (the EscalationConfigController.Save, the
   AdminStorageController precedent — the form model carries no [Required] /
   [Url] attributes); the conditional attributes below are a bare-expression
   idiom so an empty value renders **no** attribute (a bare required="" /
   checked="" would still count as present in HTML — avoided). *@
@{
    var endpointRequired = Model.Enabled ? " required" : string.Empty;
    var enabledChecked   = Model.Enabled ? " checked"  : string.Empty;
}
<form method="post" action="/admin/escalation" class="card card-body mb-4">
    @Html.AntiForgeryToken()

    @* The receiving platform endpoint (the
       escalation.outbound.endpoint.label kw-l key, the U07 closed set).
       A <input type="url">. The placeholder is the en source text as a
       literal attribute value (AGENTS.md Razor trap #3 — no <kw-l> inside a
       quoted attribute). The required= attribute is present only when
       Enabled (the bare-expression idiom above). *@
    <div class="mb-3">
        <label class="form-label" for="endpoint">
            <kw-l key="escalation.outbound.endpoint.label">Receiving platform endpoint</kw-l>
        </label>
        <input type="url" class="form-control" id="endpoint" name="Endpoint"
               value="@Model.Endpoint"
               placeholder="https://receiving.example/escalations/inbound"@endpointRequired />
        @* The endpoint help line (the escalation.outbound.endpoint.help kw-l key).
           A <div class="form-text"> below the input — the house help-text idiom
           (the BreakGlass token hint). The text is platform copy, so it goes
           through <kw-l> by key (the kw-l floor, ADR 0015 D1). *@
        <div class="form-text">
            <kw-l key="escalation.outbound.endpoint.help">The receiving instance's inbound URL — they give it to you. It is the address your escalated issues are delivered to (it ends in /escalations/inbound).</kw-l>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* The access token (the escalation.outbound.token.label kw-l key, the U07
       closed set). A <input type="password"> — the ESC·4 outgoing secret (the
       plaintext the origin admin was handed; stored on the
       EscalationOutboundConfig doc, not on the receiving side, ESC·3). The
       placeholder is the en source text as a literal attribute value (AGENTS.
       md Razor trap #3). *@
    <div class="mb-3">
        <label class="form-label" for="token">
            <kw-l key="escalation.outbound.token.label">Access token (issued by the receiving admin)</kw-l>
        </label>
        <input type="password" class="form-control" id="token" name="Token"
               value="@Model.Token"
               placeholder="kesc_…"
               autocomplete="new-password" />
        @* The token help line (the escalation.outbound.token.help kw-l key). *@
        <div class="form-text">
            <kw-l key="escalation.outbound.token.help">The access token the receiving instance's admin generated for you and shared out-of-band. It is sent as a Bearer credential so the receiving instance knows you are authorized to send.</kw-l>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* The enable switch (the escalation.outbound.enabled.label kw-l key, the
       U07 closed set). A <input type="checkbox"> — the checked= attribute is
       present only when Enabled (the bare-expression idiom above). *@
    <div class="mb-3 form-check">
        <input type="checkbox" class="form-check-input" id="enabled" name="Enabled"
               value="true"@enabledChecked />
        <label class="form-check-label" for="enabled">
            <kw-l key="escalation.outbound.enabled.label">Enable escalation</kw-l>
        </label>
        @* The enable help line (the escalation.outbound.enabled.help kw-l key). *@
        <div class="form-text">
            <kw-l key="escalation.outbound.enabled.help">Turn this on once both fields above are filled in. While it is off, escalating a report does nothing.</kw-l>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* The submit button (the escalation.outbound.save kw-l key, the U07
       closed set). *@
    <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">
        <kw-l key="escalation.outbound.save">Save escalation settings</kw-l>
    </button>
</form>
